# Line endings are law for what the suite reads as TEXT (prd-25 ruling 4, #210).
#
# The era corpus reaches @rhizomorph/core as `?raw` text through
# packages/core/src/eras/corpus.ts, and the golden-era law compares the fold to
# the committed snapshot byte for byte. Under core.autocrlf=true — the
# Git-for-Windows default — a checkout rewrites LF to CRLF, so the INPUT lies
# and nothing inside the suite can repair it: the corpus cannot be re-blessed
# from inside vitest by design (eras.test.ts). The same failure reaches every
# JSON / JSONL / text fixture a collector test reads off disk. The only native
# Windows run this project has ever done lost the eras golden byte-identity to
# exactly this.
#
# packages/server/src/corpus-eol-law.test.ts asks `git check-attr` about three
# derived sets — the `?raw` imports of corpus.ts, every non-source file under
# eras/, every collector fixture — and fails if one is not covered here. That is
# narrower than "every file the suite reads as text": the .ts.txt fixtures under
# packages/server/src/lab/__fixtures__ and the .ts sources read back through
# import.meta.glob ?raw are text the suite reads and are NOT pinned. Both were
# checked under CRLF and their laws stayed green, so the gap is scope, not a
# live defect — but the law will not catch a new text fixture outside the three.
#
# Deliberately not the blanket idiom `* text=auto eol=lf`: that rewrites
# docs/research/2026-08-04-prd10-gorgeous-spike.md, which is 239 CRLF lines in
# the index (blob a7c74b7 -> d21a88b), and renormalising a committed file is
# what #210 forbids. Nothing below renormalised a committed byte: every pinned
# file was already LF (or a single line with no line ending) in the index when
# this landed.
*.jsonl                                        text eol=lf
*.json                                         text eol=lf
packages/server/src/collectors/*/fixtures/**   text eol=lf
.swarm/*.txt                                   text eol=lf
